#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""gwcbasic.py -- the GWBASIC NATIVE COMPILER (single file).

Compiles a GW-BASIC program straight to a native Windows .exe with no
Python and no external files needed at runtime: the interpreter/parser
(gwibasic), the C code generator (genc), the C runtime (csrc/*.c +
gwruntime.h) and the MSVC build recipe are all embedded below.

    usage:  gwcbasic [--vm] [--keep-c] [--c-only] <prog.bas> [more.bas ...]

Per program: parse -> generate C -> cl.exe -> <stem>.exe written NEXT TO
the source (spawn.bas -> spawn.exe).  The compiled exe mirrors the
interpreter (gwibasic.py) bit-for-bit, including its documented quirks.

By default the NATIVE C code generator is used (nenc.py): type-specialized
direct-jump codegen, ~100x+ faster on compute-heavy programs, byte-identical
output (29/29 baseline gate).  Statements the generator can't specialize run
as gw_bridge islands through the same VM runtime, so the native path is a
strict superset of the VM path.

Options (may appear before or after the .bas files):
  --vm       use the legacy VM (bytecode interpreter) codegen instead of the
             native generator.  Diagnostic fallback only: A/B a program when
             chasing a discrepancy, or feed the VM-side parity gate.
  --keep-c   also save the generated <stem>.c next to the source
  --c-only   only generate the .c (no compile/link step)

Build recipe = the session-23-verified one: the MSVC Include dir is
FLAT (vcruntime.h lives directly in the VC Include dir).  cl.exe must
be on the same machine (see the MSVC/SDK paths below).
"""
